    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-antony.jpg" alt="Mark Antony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octavian_and_Antony_denarius_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Byzantium565, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> came under Roman rule at the end of the civil war between Octavian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. After the defeat of Antony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a> in 31 BCE, Octavian marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, which fell on 1 August 30 BCE; Antony and Cleopatra took their own lives, and Cleopatra's son by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Ptolemy XV Caesarion</a>, was put to death. Octavian, soon to be called Augustus, annexed the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, but he did not hand it to the Senate like other rich provinces. Egypt was governed by a prefect of equestrian rank, appointed by and answerable to the emperor alone, and members of the Senate and leading equestrians needed the emperor's permission to enter the country, a rule that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> invoked when he rebuked his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> for visiting in 19 CE. The first prefect, the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cornelius-gallus">Gaius Cornelius Gallus</a>, suppressed a revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> and campaigned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a>, but his boastful inscriptions led to his disgrace and suicide in 26 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> largely kept the Ptolemaic machinery of government, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> and its Greek-speaking officials, while gradually reshaping it along Roman lines and placing three legions, later reduced to two and then one, in the country.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexandria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandria.jpg" alt="Alexandria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Stefano_Grand_Plaza.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TheEgyptian, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the most productive agricultural land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and a large share of its wheat, collected as tax in kind, was shipped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> to feed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and, after 330, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Land was held by the state, by temples and, increasingly, by private owners, among them great families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apion-family">Apiones of Oxyrhynchus</a> in late antiquity. Alexandria, with perhaps half a million people, was the second city of the empire, a port linking the Mediterranean to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through harbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myos-hormos">Myos Hormos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-troglodytica">Berenike</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles and gems arrived. Society was divided by status: Roman citizens, Alexandrian citizens, Greek-speaking residents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> capitals, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> who paid a higher poll tax, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a> of 212 made almost all free inhabitants citizens. Tens of thousands of papyri, above all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> villages and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanis">Karanis</a>, record leases, taxes, lawsuits, letters and school exercises. Traditional temples kept their cults for centuries, and the painted mummy portraits of the Faiyum blend Egyptian burial with Roman portraiture. From the third century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread rapidly, and by the fifth century most Egyptians were Christian.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samo.jpg" alt="Samo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%A1klo_(vrch),_S%C3%A1mo_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Palickap, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with a relatively small garrison. Under Augustus three legions were stationed there; by the second century only one remained, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-traiana-fortis">Second Legion Traiana</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-epirus">Nicopolis</a> just east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, supported by auxiliary units and a fleet, the <em>classis Alexandrina</em>. Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> fought abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelius-gallus">Aelius Gallus</a> led a failed expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in 26–25 BCE, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a> raided the south and carried off statues of Augustus, the prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-petronius-prefect-of-egypt">Gaius Petronius</a> marched up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> in about 24 BCE, before a settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> in 21–20 BCE fixed the frontier. A bronze head of Augustus, buried beneath a temple step at Meroe, survives from that raid. Most fighting was internal: riots between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in Alexandria from 38 CE, the Jewish revolt of 115–117 that destroyed the Alexandrian Jewish community, the revolt of the herdsmen known as the Boukoloi in the Delta in the early 170s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>'s massacre of Alexandrians in 215. Egypt was lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> around 270 and recovered by Aurelian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> besieged Alexandria to crush a usurpation in 297–298 and withdrew the southern frontier to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> in the face of the Blemmyes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobades">Nobades</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septimius-severus.jpg" alt="Septimius Severus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> combined civil, judicial and military authority, assisted by officials such as the <em>iuridicus</em>, the head of the special account for irregular revenues, the <em>idios logos</em>, and the epistrategoi of the main regions. Each of the roughly forty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> was run by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a>, and village scribes registered land, people and animals for tax. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Censuses</a> were taken every fourteen years, and the state relied heavily on liturgies, compulsory public services imposed on property owners. In about 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and the nome capitals town councils, shifting tax collection and local administration onto their propertied classes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> broke up the single province and brought Egypt into the empire's common system of taxation and coinage, and in the later fourth century, probably around 381, a separate diocese of Egypt was created under an Augustal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">Prefect</a> resident in Alexandria. Justinian reorganised it again, probably in 538 or 539, with his Edict 13, which combined civil and military powers in regional governors in order to secure the grain shipments to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Alongside the state, the patriarch of Alexandria became one of the most powerful figures in the country, controlling church wealth and great crowds, and by the sixth century large estates and their owners carried out many public functions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/almagest"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almagest.jpg" alt="Almagest" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_Ptolemaeus,_Almagestum,_1515.djvu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ptolemy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> remained one of the great intellectual centres of the ancient world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a> wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a></em> and the <em>Geography</em> there in the second century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> studied under the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas">Ammonius Saccas</a> before founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hero-of-alexandria">Hero of Alexandria</a> described machines and devices. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catechetical-school-of-alexandria">Catechetical School</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-alexandria">Clement</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> made the city a centre of Christian learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s greatest contribution to later history was Christian monasticism: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-the-great">Anthony</a> withdrew into the desert in the late third century and became the model of the hermit, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachomius-the-great">Pachomius</a> founded communal monasteries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabennisi">Tabennisi</a> from the 320s, and the sayings of the desert fathers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitria">Nitria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scetis">Scetis</a> spread across the Christian world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Alexandria from 328 to 373, defended the creed of the Council of Nicaea against the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, an Alexandrian priest, and his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-antony">Life of Antony</a></em> carried the monastic ideal west. Coptic, Egyptian written in Greek letters with several signs taken from Demotic, became the language of a new literature and is still used in the liturgy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a>. The papyri preserved in Egypt's dry sands are the richest documentary source for daily life anywhere in the Roman world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesus.jpg" alt="Jesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spas_vsederzhitel_sinay_(cropped1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The church controversies of late antiquity weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>'s hold on Egyptian loyalty. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451 deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dioscorus-i-of-alexandria">Patriarch Dioscorus</a>, most Egyptian Christians rejected its definition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Christ</a>'s two natures, and two rival churches emerged: the imperial, Chalcedonian church and the Miaphysite church that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a>, each with its own patriarch from the 530s. Pagan institutions faded meanwhile: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapeum-of-alexandria">Serapeum of Alexandria</a> was destroyed in 391, the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypatia">Hypatia</a> was murdered by a Christian mob in 415, and Justinian had the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> closed in the 530s. During the last great war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in about 619, and held it as Sasanian Egypt until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> negotiated its return in 629. Heraclius's attempt to impose religious unity through the patriarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus</a>, appointed in 631, provoked persecution and resentment. In late 639 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> invaded for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, defeated the Roman army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliopolis-ancient-egypt">Heliopolis</a> in 640, and took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 641. Cyrus agreed to surrender Alexandria by treaty in November 641, and the last imperial troops left in 642. A Byzantine fleet briefly retook Alexandria in 645–646, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> recaptured it and Egypt passed permanently out of the empire.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, governed directly by the emperors for nearly seven centuries, fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> a leading centre of science and philosophy, gave the world Christian monasticism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Church</a>, and left in its papyri the fullest record of everyday life in the Roman world.</em></p>

